--[[
bkgd
   The standard background colour for large amounts of text (documents & code).  In a light theme it should
   be near-white.
inverse
   The inverse light value of bkgd (or must contrast significantly). Useful for heavy contrast against bkgd,
   e.g. stroking menu borders.
prime (primary)
   Should have a strong chroma and is used for drawing attention, like for highlighting and selection.
contrast
   A suitable complement to prime and should be of a strongly contrasting hue
neutral
   A default background colour for undefined areas (e.g. window background), usually a light grey tone
handle
   The fill for draggable elements that sit on a 'mid' track, e.g. slider knobs, scrollbar thumbs and
   splitter bars.  Should be clearly distinct from 'mid' without competing with 'prime'.
shade
   A translucent darkening overlay, e.g. modal backdrops, shadows and backing panels for light text drawn over
   arbitrary content.  Must include an alpha component and should remain dark in both light and dark themes.
error
   Indicates failure or invalid input, e.g. validation borders, error icons and messages.  Should be recognisably red
   and legible against 'bkgd'.  Dark themes need a lighter tone, so 'altText' over an 'error' fill is not guaranteed
   to be legible.
success
   Indicates a completed or valid state, e.g. confirmation icons and messages.  Should be recognisably green and
   follow the same legibility rules as 'error'.
mid
   Used for separating an area from bkgd without drawing attention.  E.g. scrollbar and slider tracks
edge
   The stroke colour for widgets
text
   The default colour for text drawn over 'bkgd'; often used for labels and sometimes stroked decorations
altText
   The default colour for text drawn over 'prime' when highlighted or selected.  Can be same as 'text'
--]]

